-- FLOAT ARITHMETIC end to end (plan 0.75 F4). Expected exit code 23
--
-- `1.5 - 2.1` was `binop left: Type mismatch: expected Int but got Float` until
-- F4. This program mixes all three operations, so recognition lifts the whole
-- expression into ONE `arith.block.<digest>` whose body is float instructions:
-- `addsd`/`mulsd`/`subsd` on x86, `fadd.d`/`fmul.d`/`fsub.d` on RISC-V.
--
-- WHAT IT CHECKS HERE is that the program builds and reaches its exit code on
-- all three targets — the block links, the instructions assemble, nothing
-- traps. The VALUE is checked, more sharply, by `FloatEmitSpec`'s "arith:"
-- group, which reads the result back out of the effect trace on every arch
-- against an expectation computed at THAT arch's precision.
--
-- A CORRECTION WORTH KEEPING. This file first said the trace check was out of
-- reach because `compose emitF@E <expression>` does not typecheck. That is
-- true and irrelevant: the blocker is `compose`, whose second argument must be
-- a MORPHISM `Unit ⇒ Float` — so a literal gets there by the value-lift and an
-- expression has no route. It is not about `emitF`, and not about floats:
-- `compose emit@E (1 + 2)` fails identically for `Int`. APPLYING the SigOp
-- (`emitF@E (…)`) has no such requirement and the effect is entered, which is
-- exactly what `FloatEmitSpec` now does. What is never entered is a
-- `let _ = … in` binding — a third shape, and the one the older comments in
-- this directory are about.

import I.Linux.Syscalls as S
import I.Test.Emit as E

emitLit : IO Unit
emitLit = emitF@E (1.5 + 2.25 * 2.0 - 0.5)

main : IO Unit
main = compose exit@S (compose 23 emitLit)
